That may be the point, but I think it also says something. Scrolls of
genocide are essentially one way of giving the player power to modify
the game and remove or minimize some parts they don't like.

I don't mind playing a game with teleporting undead summoning
spellcasters who'd destroy your armor and possibly instakill you if
you don't have MR. That's one of the few points of the vanilla game I
found interesting through Gehenom.

I'm exceptionally wary of playing with teleporting undead summoning
spellcasters who'll weaken/destroy your armor even if you have MR.
There are other parts of the game that I might carp about or suggest
different ideas for, but this is one of two things that so far I
really actually dislike, pretty much for the reasons the original
poster mentioned. I think it does force you to make a choice between
(a) the tedium of pulling together 2-3 fully ready armor kits (b)
modifying the game via ?oG to minimize the encounters (c) enjoying the
brisk feeling of terror, exhileration, and focus that the process of
involuntary exhibitionism during critical moments can bring. :)

I suppose there's always (e) becoming strong enough to instakill,
missile kill, or distance all teleporting spellcasters within the time
they bring themselves in range or (d) gaming the protection via prayer
system down to -30 naked AC without the benefits of pudding farming.
Neither is impossible, but both are not infrequently going to fail to
happen by the time the first Master Lich shows up.

My idea of balance for this situation would be to make blessed
confused scrolls of enchant armor automatically pick damaged armor to
repair. This would mitigate the problem without making it trivial.
Getting the extra scrolls isn't (usually) hard, especially as the game
goes on, but it's one more thing to do and several more things to
carry, and if you want to fix your armor in an emergency, you'd still
to have to spend at least two turns confused, which may not be
convenient and in some cases it might not even be fast enough to deal
with an emerging situation and you'd lose armor anyway (or be forced
to choose between losing armor and something more serious). But at
least you wouldn't have to get out of melee and remove everything but
the damaged item first.

For the moment, though, I'll just be keeping Liches out of my games
when I can.



